          "question": "I can only get a few words out before I have to stop for air, and my rescue puffer isn't doing anything tonight.",
          "answer": "**Please get emergency help now. Call your local emergency number (for example 911 in the US or 999 in the UK), or have someone drive you to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait to see if it passes.**\n\nBeing able to speak only a few words before stopping for breath, while your rescue inhaler is not working, is a sign of severe breathing difficulty. The guidance I found says that trouble speaking is one of the most useful signs of how serious a breathing problem is (Severe difficulty breathing, emergency medicine and respiratory society guidance). It also warns that feeling calm or getting drowsy while working hard to breathe is not a sign of getting better. Please do not rely on a finger oxygen reading to decide whether to go in.\n\nThe automatic check did not match this to a specific emergency pattern. That does not mean it is safe to wait. Treat this as an emergency.\n\nThings I could not cover from the sources I searched: what to do while you wait for help, and what treatment you would get. Please ask the emergency team or your clinician about that.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
